Description: network quality tray indicator for interactive use
 A system tray indicator that continuously probes one or more targets and
 grades the connection as good, fair, poor, bad or down, showing coloured
 signal bars in the panel.
 .
 Unlike throughput monitors, it measures what makes an interactive session
 usable: every probe is classified OK, LATE or LOST against a configurable
 deadline, so a reply that arrives too late to be acted on is counted as an
 impairment rather than a success. It also tracks the longest run of
 consecutive bad probes, which distinguishes a frozen session from scattered
 packet loss at the same overall rate.
 .
 What counts as too late depends on the traffic, so a profile (ssh, realtime or
 web) sets the deadline, the thresholds and the wording together.
 .
 The probe rate adapts, staying slow while the link is healthy and speeding up
 only when it becomes unstable, which keeps it usable on metered mobile
 connections.
 .
 Several targets can be watched at once. The first sets the icon and the
 grade; the others are probed only during an alert, so they cost nothing
 while the link is healthy, and they show whether a fault is one destination
 or all of them. The first hop towards each target is probed as well, to show
 whether a fault is on the local link or past it. A tunnel needs no
 configuration: it is simply a first hop that leaves by a tunnel device, and
 is treated accordingly.
